  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 950 PM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThe main concern over the next 24-48 hours will be a weak \nshortwave tracking over portions of the area. The moisture we \nhave in place is already modest, but the wave is going to bring \na slight uptick (PWAT 175-215 percent of normal). It will also \nsupply some lift to aid convective development, which should \ntranslate to widespread coverage. The 00z GJT sounding revealed \nsteep midlevel lapse and those will still be in place tomorrow. \nThe lapse rates and moisture combo should result in CAPE around \n1000 j/kg. Given this environment storms tomorrow will be \ncapable of producing strong winds, hail and heavy rainfall. The \nheavy rain may result in flash flooding and debris flows \nespecially around steep terrain and recent burn scars. This \npotential is for most locations across the area. Many spots have\nbeen getting heavy rainfall the past several days, which has \nlead to saturated soils. This further increases the potential \nfor excessive runoff. If this increased moisture leads to \nexpansive cloud cover that lingers through mid day tomorrow then\nthe atmosphere will not be as unstable. This could ultimately \ncause a decrease in coverage and intensity of storms. Therefore \nheld off on issuing a flash flood watch, but it is certainly \npossible especially if the skies are clear in late morning. \nModels show the shortwave trough still tracking overhead \nWednesday night. This could support showers and storms \ncontinuing overnight. Although by late evening the rainfall \nrates should be on the decrease. \n\nOn Thursday the trough crosses the Divide at some point leaving\nbehind westerly flow. Plenty of moisture will still be around \nfor peak heating, so it looks like terrain-driven storms are \nlikely in the afternoon. Despite some dry air behind the wave \nthe moisture values still support the potential for heavy \nrainfall. On Friday northwest flow will be in place and models \nhint at dry air trying to filter into the area. When that \nhappens is a pretty good bet the coverage decreases. Although \nlow level moisture is usually slow to filter out, so it should \nsupport another round of terrain-driven storms in the afternoon.\nBehind that the confidence lowers as models are not sure how to\nhandle the pool of moisture. How far south does it go and when \nwill return flow bring it back into the area? Is there enough \nlingering moisture to keep orographic showers going into the \nweekend? Stay tuned",
